hi there Steve Goffin here again to talk  about language learning which is a  subject that I find fascinating that's  become a big part of my life the last 15  years or so and today I want to talk  about a few subjects that are related in  my mind it has to do with intermediate  content which is a major problem for  language learners there's a lot of  beginner content and it's easy to access  authentic content newspapers you know  Netflix videos but it's difficult to get  from the beginner content where  obviously there's a lot of  high-frequency verb or words from there  to being able to actually enjoy the  authentic content so how do we fill the  gap what I'm finding in my Persian is  that I've asked this Iranian or several  Iranians to create stories or to create  episodes where they talk about  themselves who they are what they like  to do their professional life what they  studied their interests in different  sports or cooking or whatever it might  be to speak naturally and then to  transcribe I've mentioned this before  and I think we're gonna get some  dialogues as well where people just talk  to each other and then transcribe so  this is natural content but one of the  advantages of the many stories which is  where I you know start into any language  is the amount of repetition because we  have the sort of point of view different  tense or different person  covering essentially the same story and  then followed by questions and the  questions of course are simply more  exposure because they begin with a  statement of something from the story  and then they ask you to say where why  or yes or no without forcing you to try  and think about what happened in the  story which is something that I find  quite annoying because I sometimes can't  remember all I really want is that you  know three times a triple repetition of  essentially this  same vocabulary with some change you  know going from a statement to a  question or positive to negative and so  forth so that works really well so I've  asked Safra who is my Iranian  collaborator to create questions for  these you know rambles of hers where she  talks about herself and what she likes  to do and it really improves them  because it makes it easier to focus on  the vocabulary in the original monologue  and it's easier to understand the  original monologue so it drives you back  into the story I've mentioned this  before I would like to get people to  help me figure out how we can create  this kind of content we have a series in  link called the Iranians can we have one  for I don't know the the Parisians the  québécois the Brazilians that the  Pekinese I don't know the Russians  anything where a group of people can get  together record a 5-minute little  monologue or a dialogue then create ten  questions in this format statement  question about the statement and then  answer repeating the same vocabulary we  will be I will be happy to compensate  people I would love to start getting  this kind of content into link and/or we  make it available to people we would use  it in link but other people can use it  however they want so I would be very  interested in doing that now people have  pointed out that this is a technique  called circling or it relates to a  technique called circling the point of  view stories the circling all of these  things come from a variety of teaching  techniques in the sort of t p RS  teaching proficiency through reading and  storytelling and there are variants on  this theme it's the sort of  comprehensible input approach to  language learning now I have I'm gonna  leave two links in the description box  two articles that describe this circling  questions technique and just the two  that I look at in preparation for this  video and which I you know I'm leaving  the links here in the description box  this very much describes a teaching  technique now my perspective as a  learner as an independent learner and  when I look at other independent  learners who are mostly 18 years and  older you know our situation and our  needs as learners our needs are  different from the position of a teacher  who has to amuse the classroom I mean I  remember as a kid in class we were just  as much interested in interacting with  our friends in the classroom as in  listening to the teacher so the teacher  has to grab our attention somehow and in  one of the links you'll see there's a  video of an example of a circling you  know question type class for Spanish but  but typically the the emphasis there the  teacher has to teach something so the  teacher uses these circling questions to  help you know give the learners more  exposure more repetition for certain  specific structures that they're trying  to get across it's almost like a form of  drilling it's trying to drill home  certain structures so that keeps the  kids occupied and undoubtedly teaches  them something but that's not what I'm  looking for my experience as a learner  is that if I'm being drilled in a  certain structure or even if circling  questions are being used to provide that  repetition that necessary repetition in  a structure my brain starts that you  know tune out and that very often these  structures that I want to learn I'm  going to learn them over a long period  of time by coming across those  structures in different scenarios in  different bits of content so that my  goal is to simply consume a lot of  content confident that eventually the  structures are gonna are gonna you know  fall into place I might occasionally  look up  explanations or see examples you know  concentrated examples of these  structures but mostly I'm relying on my  listening and reading to you know make  it possible for my brain to start to tie  some of these ends together and  recognize these these patterns so what  I'm looking for is not so much the sort  of something that would captivate a  classroom of little kids but rather  stories monologues that are in of  themselves interesting so we're meeting  one and then Jose and then Maria and  we're meeting different people from  other know Venezuela or you know Peru  who talk about who they are and what  they do which is interesting because if  we're interested in the language we're  interested in the people and so these  are natural monologues or dialogues but  then to make sure we can better  understand these stories we follow up  with these say ten or twelve questions  and so that it's it's not so much  listening to a teacher in the classroom  to try and nail down some structure it's  rather natural you know input  comprehensible interesting input made  more comprehensible because we have  these follow-up questions which actually  don't require us to think of anything is  just more exposure to the language so  have a look at the have a look at the  the links there just to understand this  this technique which is used with great  success I think in classrooms but what I  would really like to know is if anyone  can send me an email steve at link calm  if you can get together with some people  whether it be at your university place  of work a bunch of friends and we will  compensate you might be money might be  membership names i don't know but they  have to be good like they have to be  good it can't be you know you've got a  list of ten things my sports my food my  this and it sort of becomes a humdrum  you know going through the motions the  idea is to get interesting  almost like many biographies of people  in the language group you know whose  language we're learning and then to help  us understand these better we add these  questions at the end  and if we can get lots of these I think  that's a very good sort of intermediate  type of content it becomes intermediate  because when people speak naturally they  tend to use less difficult words it  could be compelling because we learn  about different people we have to meet  different people and it can become more  comprehensible if we have these  questions at the end and I think that'll  kind of move us towards better you know  to becoming better at using authentic  content although I would also like to  try you know if people are willing to  help us you know provide these kinds of  questions for you know genuine authentic  you know material like transcribe  podcast and stuff of that nature but  anyway this is what I'm interested in  seeing how far we explore this idea I  look forward to hearing from you Steve  at MIT calm  [Music]
 

